(6565, 'Ravi Shankar (Bengali pronunciation: [ˈrobi ˈʃɔŋkor], born Robindro Shaunkor Chowdhury, sometimes spelled as Rabindra Shankar Chowdhury, 7 April 1920 – 11 December 2012) was an Indian sitarist and composer. A sitar virtuoso, he became the world''s best-known expert of Indian classical music in the second half of the 20th century, and influenced many musicians in India and throughout the world. Shankar was awarded India''s highest civilian honour, the Bharat Ratna, in 1999. He is also the father of American singer Norah Jones and British-American musician and sitar  player Anoushka Shankar.
Shankar was born to a Bengali family in India, and spent his youth as a dancer touring India and Europe with the dance group of his brother Uday Shankar. At age 18, he gave up dancing to pursue a career in music, studying the sitar for seven years under court musician Allauddin Khan. After finishing his studies in 1944, Shankar worked as a composer, creating the music for the Apu Trilogy by Satyajit Ra', NULL, 0),
